  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Katya tells Denis that it is already too late to turn back, Chef Barinov rallies Senya, Fedya, Lyova, and Louis in the corridor. He sends them into the kitchen for a thirty-minute cook-off to reclaim their jobs.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Парни, сейчас всё в ваших руках.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Покажите, кто на кухне хозяин.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Идите и верните свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Шеф, мы их сегодня порвём!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Мы быстро победим этих поваров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лучше докажи это на кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте как в старые времена!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Каждая пара приготовит одно блюдо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто приготовит лучше, тот останется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лучшие останутся на этой кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас ровно полчаса времени.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте, ребята, приступайте к работе!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Парни, сейчас всё в ваших руках.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Guys, everything is in your hands right now.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'guys' and finishing on 'hands'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Покажите, кто на кухне хозяин.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Show them who is the master in the kitchen.' Notice how Russian expresses 'to show' and 'owner' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Идите и верните свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go and win back your jobs.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'go' before landing on 'work'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Шеф, мы их сегодня порвём!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, we will tear them apart today!' In Russian, the idea of 'Chef Barinov' flows naturally into 'we will tear apart' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Мы быстро победим этих поваров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will quickly defeat these cooks.' Fedya anchors the line on 'we', while 'cooks' completes the thought. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лучше докажи это на кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Better prove that in the kitchen.' The grammar packs 'better' and 'kitchen' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте как в старые времена!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us do it like in the old days!' The grammar packs 'let's' and 'time' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Каждая пара приготовит одно блюдо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Each pair will prepare one dish.' In Russian, the idea of 'every' flows naturally into 'dish' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто приготовит лучше, тот останется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Whoever cooks better will stay.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'who' before landing on 'remains'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лучшие останутся на этой кухне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The best will remain in this kitchen.' The meaning hinges on 'best' working together with 'kitchen' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас ровно полчаса времени.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have exactly half an hour.' The meaning hinges on 'at' working together with 'time' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте, ребята, приступайте к работе!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, guys, get started on the work!' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'let's' and 'guys' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
